{"id": "sdqa0152", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 2.8, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1808, "eot_read_ms": 17, "reference_answer": "in the May 1971 Creem, Saunders wrote, \"Sir Lord Baltimore seems to have down pat most all the best heavy metal tricks in the book\".[99] Creem critic Lester Bangs is credited with popularizing the term via his early 1970s essays on bands such as Led Zeppelin and Black Sabbath", "query": "Why is heavy metal called heavy metal?", "transcript": "Why is heavy metal called heavy metal?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Heavy metal is called heavy metal because the music has a very loud and powerful sound. The guitars often use a lot of distortion, which makes the music sound thick and heavy. Also, the themes in heavy metal songs can be dark or intense, like about anger, rebellion, or fantasy—so both the sound and the mood feel \"heavy.\" It all started as a style of rock music that sounded much heavier than other types of music at the time.", "answer_ms": 1797}
